// =============================================================================
// Surfaces: where a band was measured, at whatever scale the paper said.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// This file is JSONC (JSON with Comments). The build step strips // and /* */
// comments before shipping clean JSON to the browser.
//
// ONE TABLE, THREE LEVELS
// "Where was this measured" does not have one answer, it has a scale. A paper
// might say Cu⁺, or TiO₂, or Pt/TiO₂, and all three are true at once. Rather
// than forcing each into a separate table and then arguing about which one
// TiO₂ belongs in, every entry here is a surface with a `level` saying how
// specific it is:
//
//   site    the atom-scale spot the molecule is bonded to: a cation, a
//           reduced metal atom, a vacancy, a proton, or a named ensemble of
//           those. This is what "site" means strictly.
//   phase   a constituent of a sample, named by composition: TiO₂, Al₂O₃,
//           ZnO. Strictly the molecule sits on some site *within* the phase
//           (on TiO₂ it is a Ti⁴⁺ or a surface O), but papers very often
//           report only the phase, and recording that honestly beats guessing.
//   sample  what was actually in the cell as a made thing: Cu/ZnO,
//           Ru/Al₂O₃, Cu-GaZrOₓ. A bare oxide is a phase even when it is
//           the only thing in the cell, facet and all: Fe₃O₄(001) is a phase.
//
// The same entry can play more than one role without changing: TiO₂ is the
// sample when a paper measures bare titania, and the phase when the same
// titania is the support in Pt/TiO₂. That ambiguity is in the chemistry, not
// in the schema, so the schema does not try to resolve it.
//
// `parts` is the one containment link, and it points down the scale: a sample
// lists its phases and the sites it presents, a phase lists the sites within
// it, a composite site lists the simpler sites it is built from. It is what
// lets a query for Cu⁺ reach the bands whose paper only named Cu/ZnO, and
// what would let a query for Ti⁴⁺ reach the ones that only named TiO₂ (add
// the cation as a site entry and list it under the phase to say so).
//
// The one exception is an interface site (kind: interface), which may name a
// phase among its parts: the Pt⁰-CeO₂ perimeter is not a Pt atom plus a Ce
// cation, it is the metal against the oxide, so the phase is what it is made
// of. build.py rejects a coarser part anywhere else.
//
// A band reference names surfaces in ONE field:
//   measured_on — one or more keys from this table, at any level. A paper
//                 that named both the site and the sample gets both, a paper
//                 that named only the catalyst gets one, and neither is a
//                 lesser record than the other.
//
// IDS ARE ASCII, LABELS CARRY THE UNICODE
// Same convention as band ids, group keys and species keys: the key is a
// machine identifier (`cu_1p`), the label is what a reader sees (`Cu⁺`).
// Charge is spelled out as `_0`, `_1p`, `_2p` and so on. Element-symbol ids in
// this file always mean the element (`co_0` is cobalt metal, never carbon
// monoxide).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SCHEMA
//   <key>:             ASCII identifier, referenced by
//                        band.references[].measured_on
//     label:           display text, Unicode (required)
//     level:           site | phase | sample (required). It decides how the
//                        badge is drawn: a site is filled, a phase or sample
//                        is hollow, so the scale is visible at a glance.
//     kind:            for a site only: metal | cation | defect | interface |
//                        bronsted (optional)
//     element:         element symbol, for an atom-scale site (optional)
//     oxidation_state: integer, for metal/cation sites (optional)
//     formula:         Unicode formula, for a phase (optional)
//     composition:     the sample as the literature writes it, for a sample
//                        (optional). A phase uses `formula` instead, facet or
//                        no facet: a cut crystal is the same compound.
//     elements:        every element this surface contains, as symbols. What
//                        answers "anything on a Cu-containing sample",
//                        whichever level the claim named (optional for a
//                        site, where `element` already says it)
//     facet:           crystallographic termination, for a single crystal.
//                        It narrows a phase; it never sits on a site (optional)
//     parts:           keys of the surfaces one scale down (optional). See
//                        above: this is the only containment link.
//     note:            one sentence, shown on the Dataset page (optional)
//
// KEEP IN SYNC: adding, renaming or removing a key here means updating
// data/bands.jsonc in the same change. build.py fails on an unresolved key.
// =============================================================================
{
  "surfaces": {

    // ── Sites: the atom-scale spot a molecule is bonded to ──
    "cu_0": {
      "label": "Cu⁰",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "metal",
      "element": "Cu",
      "oxidation_state": 0,
      "note": "Metallic copper. Carries ν(CO) roughly 40–80 cm⁻¹ below the Cu⁺ carbonyl."
    },
    "ag_0": {
      "label": "Ag⁰",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "metal",
      "element": "Ag",
      "oxidation_state": 0,
      "note": "Metallic silver. Binds atomic hydrogen weakly and formate strongly enough, which is the combination that gives it the lowest barrier to formate of the three coinage metals."
    },
    "au_0": {
      "label": "Au⁰",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "metal",
      "element": "Au",
      "oxidation_state": 0,
      "note": "Metallic gold. Binds formate too weakly to accumulate it, the opposite failure to copper, which binds hydrogen too strongly."
    },
    "ag_sio2_interface": {
      "label": "Ag⁰-SiO₂ perimeter",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "interface",
      "parts": ["ag_0", "sio2"],
      "note": "The rim where a silver nanoparticle meets the silica, named as the active site for methyl formate: the formate sits on the metal and the carbonic acid and adsorbed methanol on the silica beside it, so the esterification happens where the two meet rather than on either alone."
    },
    "pd_0": {
      "label": "Pd⁰",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "metal",
      "element": "Pd",
      "oxidation_state": 0
    },
    "co_0": {
      "label": "Co⁰",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "metal",
      "element": "Co",
      "oxidation_state": 0
    },
    "pt_0": {
      "label": "Pt⁰",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "metal",
      "element": "Pt",
      "oxidation_state": 0
    },
    "ru_0": {
      "label": "Ru⁰",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "metal",
      "element": "Ru",
      "oxidation_state": 0
    },
    "ga": {
      "label": "Ga",
      "level": "site",
      "element": "Ga",
      "parts": ["ga_tet", "ga_oct"],
      "note": "A Ga centre carrying a hydride, written as Ga-H with no charge in the sources that report it. Neither Ga⁰ nor Ga³⁺ is asserted, which is why this entry has no kind and no oxidation state; use ga_3p only where the paper states the cation. The two coordinations below are the resolved forms of it: a claim that says only Ga is a claim that did not separate them."
    },
    "ga_tet": {
      "label": "Ga(t)",
      "level": "site",
      "element": "Ga",
      "note": "Tetrahedrally coordinated gallium, the Ga(t) of β-Ga₂O₃. Its hydride and hydroxyl sit about 25 and 150 cm⁻¹ above those of the octahedral site, which is how the two are told apart."
    },
    "ga_oct": {
      "label": "Ga(o)",
      "level": "site",
      "element": "Ga",
      "note": "Octahedrally coordinated gallium, the Ga(o) of β-Ga₂O₃. Coordinatively unsaturated at the surface, which is why it, and not the saturated tetrahedral site, is where H₂ dissociates in the calculations."
    },
    "cu_1p": {
      "label": "Cu⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "Cu",
      "oxidation_state": 1,
      "note": "The carbonyl marker that separates a partly oxidised copper surface from Cu⁰."
    },
    "cu_2p": {
      "label": "Cu²⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "Cu",
      "oxidation_state": 2,
      "note": "Fully oxidised copper, carrying a carbonyl some 25 cm⁻¹ above the Cu⁺ one. Rare in this dataset, because a working methanol catalyst is reduced: where a source reports it on one, it is claiming a minority site that survived the reduction, and the claim is worth reading against the Cu⁺ assignment it sits next to."
    },
    "zn_2p": {
      "label": "Zn²⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "Zn",
      "oxidation_state": 2
    },
    "zr_4p": {
      "label": "Zr⁴⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "Zr",
      "oxidation_state": 4
    },
    "hf_4p": {
      "label": "Hf⁴⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "Hf",
      "oxidation_state": 4
    },
    "ga_3p": {
      "label": "Ga³⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "Ga",
      "oxidation_state": 3
    },
    "in_3p": {
      "label": "In³⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "In",
      "oxidation_state": 3
    },
    "li_1p": {
      "label": "Li⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "Li",
      "oxidation_state": 1
    },
    "na_1p": {
      "label": "Na⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "Na",
      "oxidation_state": 1
    },
    "k_1p": {
      "label": "K⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "K",
      "oxidation_state": 1
    },
    "rb_1p": {
      "label": "Rb⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "Rb",
      "oxidation_state": 1
    },
    "cs_1p": {
      "label": "Cs⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "Cs",
      "oxidation_state": 1
    },
    "h_bronsted": {
      "label": "H⁺ (Brønsted)",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "bronsted",
      "element": "H",
      "note": "A proton-donating surface hydroxyl, as opposed to the Lewis-acidic cations above."
    },
    "o_vac": {
      "label": "Vₒ",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "defect",
      "note": "A bare oxygen vacancy. Listed on its own so ensembles can point at it."
    },
    "o_vac_1p": {
      "label": "Vₒ⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "defect",
      "parts": ["o_vac"],
      "note": "An oxygen vacancy that has given up one of its two electrons, the donor centre whose photoionization to the conduction band is the broad electronic absorption under an operando Cu/ZnO spectrum. Its concentration, not just its existence, is what the band measures."
    },
    "zn_ovac_hf": {
      "label": "Zn–Vₒ–Hf",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "defect",
      "parts": ["zn_2p", "o_vac", "hf_4p"],
      "note": "An oxygen vacancy flanked by Zn and Hf: the two cations and the vacancy act as one site."
    },
    "pt0_ceo2_interface": {
      "label": "Pt⁰-CeO₂ interface",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "interface",
      "parts": ["pt_0", "ceo2"],
      "note": "The perimeter where the metal particle meets the reducible support, not either phase alone."
    },

    // ── Phases: a constituent of a sample, named by composition ──
    "al2o3": {
      "label": "Al₂O₃",
      "level": "phase",
      "formula": "Al₂O₃",
      "elements": ["Al", "O"]
    },
    "tio2": {
      "label": "TiO₂",
      "level": "phase",
      "formula": "TiO₂",
      "elements": ["Ti", "O"]
    },
    "ceo2": {
      "label": "CeO₂",
      "level": "phase",
      "formula": "CeO₂",
      "elements": ["Ce", "O"]
    },
    "zno": {
      "label": "ZnO",
      "level": "phase",
      "formula": "ZnO",
      "elements": ["Zn", "O"],
      "parts": ["zn_2p"]
    },
    "zno_1mx": {
      "label": "ZnO₁₋ₓ",
      "level": "phase",
      "formula": "ZnO₁₋ₓ",
      "elements": ["Zn", "O"],
      "parts": ["zn_2p", "o_vac", "o_vac_1p"],
      "note": "Zinc oxide after reduction, carrying the oxygen vacancies that stoichiometric ZnO does not. Recorded apart from zno because the distinction is the point wherever it is named: the defects are what the strong metal-support interaction with copper creates, and what the infrared background reports on."
    },
    "zro2": {
      "label": "ZrO₂",
      "level": "phase",
      "formula": "ZrO₂",
      "elements": ["Zr", "O"],
      "parts": ["zr_4p"]
    },
    "hfo2": {
      "label": "HfO₂",
      "level": "phase",
      "formula": "HfO₂",
      "elements": ["Hf", "O"],
      "parts": ["hf_4p"],
      "note": "Hafnia, ZrO₂'s chemical twin. Monoclinic when precipitated and calcined on its own; the Zn-doped solid solutions are cubic and are the znhfox sample, not this."
    },
    "ga2o3": {
      "label": "Ga₂O₃",
      "level": "phase",
      "formula": "Ga₂O₃",
      "elements": ["Ga", "O"],
      "parts": ["ga_3p", "ga_tet", "ga_oct"],
      "note": "Gallia, as a constituent of a promoted catalyst and as a sample in its own right. The β polymorph carries both gallium coordinations, which is why hydride and hydroxyl bands on it come in pairs."
    },
    "sio2": {
      "label": "SiO₂",
      "level": "phase",
      "formula": "SiO₂",
      "elements": ["Si", "O"]
    },
    "fe2o3": {
      "label": "Fe₂O₃",
      "level": "phase",
      "formula": "Fe₂O₃",
      "elements": ["Fe", "O"]
    },
    "fe3o4": {
      "label": "Fe₃O₄",
      "level": "phase",
      "formula": "Fe₃O₄",
      "elements": ["Fe", "O"],
      "note": "Magnetite with no termination stated. The two facet variants below point at it, and all three are the same compound: only the cut differs."
    },
    "fe3o4_001": {
      "label": "Fe₃O₄(001)",
      "level": "phase",
      "formula": "Fe₃O₄",
      "facet": "(001)",
      "elements": ["Fe", "O"],
      "parts": ["fe3o4"],
      "note": "Magnetite cut on the (001) plane. The formula is the whole chemistry: (001) is a termination, not a composition, so this entry and fe3o4_111 are the same compound differently cut. It is a phase for that reason, named by formula like any other bare oxide; `parts` points at plain fe3o4 so a query for magnetite reaches it."
    },
    "fe3o4_111": {
      "label": "Fe₃O₄(111)",
      "level": "phase",
      "formula": "Fe₃O₄",
      "facet": "(111)",
      "elements": ["Fe", "O"],
      "parts": ["fe3o4"],
      "note": "The other termination measured in the same study, chemically identical to fe3o4_001. They are separate entries because the two cuts expose different Fe spacings and carry different formate geometries, which is a structural difference and not a compositional one."
    },

    // ── Samples: what was actually in the cell ──
    "cu_sio2": {
      "label": "Cu/SiO₂",
      "level": "sample",
      "composition": "Cu/SiO₂",
      "elements": ["Cu", "Si", "O"],
      "parts": ["cu_0", "sio2"],
      "note": "Copper on silica with no promoter. Silica is close to inert here, so this is the reference against which a promoter's effect is read, and it is listed with Cu⁰ alone: the sources that pair it with a promoted counterpart find no Cu⁺ carbonyl on it at all."
    },
    "ag_sio2": {
      "label": "Ag/SiO₂",
      "level": "sample",
      "composition": "Ag/SiO₂",
      "elements": ["Ag", "Si", "O"],
      "parts": ["ag_0", "ag_sio2_interface", "sio2"],
      "note": "Silver nanoparticles under 11 nm at 1 wt % on a 155 m² g⁻¹ silica, by wetness impregnation. Silica is chosen as a neutral support so the metal is what differs; it makes no methyl formate without the metal present."
    },
    "au_sio2": {
      "label": "Au/SiO₂",
      "level": "sample",
      "composition": "Au/SiO₂",
      "elements": ["Au", "Si", "O"],
      "parts": ["au_0", "sio2"],
      "note": "Gold nanoparticles under 11 nm at 1 wt % on silica, prepared alongside the Cu and Ag counterparts so the three coinage metals can be compared on one support."
    },
    "cuga_sio2": {
      "label": "Cu-Ga₂O₃/SiO₂",
      "level": "sample",
      "composition": "Cu-Ga₂O₃/SiO₂",
      "elements": ["Cu", "Ga", "Si", "O"],
      "parts": ["cu_0", "cu_1p", "cu_2p", "ga_3p", "ga2o3", "sio2"],
      "note": "Ga₂O₃-promoted Cu on silica, published as a loading series written CuGa(x) where x is the Ga/(Cu+Ga) molar ratio. The family is recorded here and the member named in the note, because the distinction the spectra turn on is against the Ga-free cu_sio2 rather than between members: the Cu⁺ carbonyl is absent without Ga and its area grows with the loading."
    },
    "cu_zno": {
      "label": "Cu/ZnO",
      "level": "sample",
      "composition": "Cu/ZnO",
      "elements": ["Cu", "Zn", "O"],
      "parts": ["cu_0", "cu_1p", "zn_2p", "zno", "zno_1mx"]
    },
    "cu_gazrox": {
      "label": "Cu-GaZrOₓ",
      "level": "sample",
      "composition": "Cu-GaZrOₓ",
      "elements": ["Cu", "Ga", "Zr", "O"],
      "parts": ["cu_0", "cu_1p", "ga", "ga_3p", "zr_4p"],
      "note": "Coprecipitated Cu-Ga-Zr mixed oxide, around 20 wt% Cu. Sources publish it as a series with different Ga and Zr loadings, written Cu-GaZrOₓ-Z where Z is Zr/(Zr+Cu+Ga) as a molar percentage; the family is recorded here and the individual member named in the note, since the distinction the spectra turn on is against the Cu-free GaZrOₓ rather than between members."
    },
    "znhfox": {
      "label": "ZnHfOₓ",
      "level": "sample",
      "composition": "ZnHfOₓ",
      "elements": ["Zn", "Hf", "O"],
      "parts": ["zn_2p", "hf_4p", "zn_ovac_hf"],
      "note": "Co-precipitated Zn–Hf mixed oxide, published as a series written xZnHfOₓ with x the Zn/(Zn+Hf) molar percentage. Up to about 35 mol% it is Zn-doped cubic HfO₂ in solid solution; above 40 mol% bulk ZnO segregates and carries HfOₓ clusters on its surface. One key for the family, with the member named in the note, because the IR bands of both regimes agree within a few wavenumbers and that agreement is the claim the spectra turn on. ZnO is not listed as a part: the low-Zn members contain none."
    },
    "gazrox": {
      "label": "GaZrOₓ",
      "level": "sample",
      "composition": "GaZrOₓ",
      "elements": ["Ga", "Zr", "O"],
      "parts": ["ga", "ga_3p", "zr_4p"],
      "note": "The Cu-free counterpart of the Cu-GaZrOₓ series, 48 wt% Ga and 25 wt% Zr. Run to separate what the oxide does from what Cu does."
    },
    "la_al2o3": {
      "label": "La-Al₂O₃",
      "level": "sample",
      "composition": "La-Al₂O₃",
      "elements": ["La", "Al", "O"],
      "parts": ["al2o3"],
      "note": "Lanthanum-doped alumina; the doping stabilises the support rather than adding an IR-visible site."
    },
    "ni_ce_fe_la_al2o3": {
      "label": "Ni-Ce-Fe/La-Al₂O₃",
      "level": "sample",
      "composition": "Ni-Ce-Fe/La-Al₂O₃",
      "elements": ["Ni", "Ce", "Fe", "La", "Al", "O"],
      "parts": ["al2o3", "ceo2"]
    },
    "ru_al2o3": {
      "label": "Ru/Al₂O₃",
      "level": "sample",
      "composition": "Ru/Al₂O₃",
      "elements": ["Ru", "Al", "O"],
      "parts": ["ru_0", "al2o3"]
    },
    "ru_na2o_al2o3": {
      "label": "Ru/\"Na₂O\"/Al₂O₃",
      "level": "sample",
      "composition": "Ru/\"Na₂O\"/Al₂O₃",
      "elements": ["Ru", "Na", "Al", "O"],
      "parts": ["ru_0", "al2o3", "na_1p"],
      "note": "The sodium-promoted counterpart of Ru/Al₂O₃; the quotes are the authors' own."
    },
    "co_al2o3": {
      "label": "Co/Al₂O₃",
      "level": "sample",
      "composition": "Co/γ-Al₂O₃",
      "elements": ["Co", "Al", "O"],
      "parts": ["co_0", "al2o3"]
    },
    "co_siralox": {
      "label": "Co/Siralox",
      "level": "sample",
      "composition": "Co/Siralox",
      "elements": ["Co", "Si", "Al", "O"],
      "parts": ["co_0", "al2o3"],
      "note": "Cobalt on Siralox, a commercial silica-alumina of 5% SiO₂ and 95% Al₂O₃. Kept apart from co_al2o3: the support differs, and so does the carbonyl position reported on it. SiO₂ is not listed as a part: at 5% it is a dopant of the alumina, not a phase of its own."
    },
    "zsm5": {
      "label": "ZSM-5",
      "level": "phase",
      "formula": "ZSM-5",
      "elements": ["Si", "Al", "O"],
      "note": "The MFI aluminosilicate framework, named as a phase because the cation it hosts is the site and the framework is what that site sits in. Deliberately no `parts`: the exchanged cation sits in the framework without being a constituent of it, and one sample carries one cation, so listing Li, Na, K, Rb, Cs, H and Cu together would describe a material that does not exist. A claim names both keys instead, as in [\"li_1p\", \"zsm5\"]. The Si/Al ratio decides how many cations there are and how far apart, so sources state it (14 for the alkali-exchanged series, 25 for the Cu-exchanged one) and the claim's note carries it."
    },
    "moox": {
      "label": "MoOₓ",
      "level": "phase",
      "formula": "MoOₓ",
      "elements": ["Mo", "O"],
      "note": "Sub-stoichiometric molybdenum oxide, written with x rather than a fixed stoichiometry because the reduced, oxygen-deficient form is the point: it is the H₂-reduced state that carries the vacancies, and CO₂ re-oxidises it."
    },
    "pt_moox_tio2": {
      "label": "Pt/MoOₓ/TiO₂",
      "level": "sample",
      "composition": "Pt/MoOₓ/TiO₂",
      "elements": ["Pt", "Mo", "Ti", "O"],
      "parts": ["pt_0", "moox", "tio2"],
      "note": "Platinum on a molybdenum-oxide-covered titania, made by sequential impregnation and reduced in H₂ at 300 °C. Both metals are needed: neither Pt on bare TiO₂ nor MoOₓ/TiO₂ alone gives the methanol yield, which is what the sample entry is here to keep together."
    }
  }
}
